## Fatigue

Tiredness after a stroke is very common and is not like normal tiredness. Plan rest, pace yourself, and build up slowly.

## Communication

If you have **aphasia**, give yourself time, use gestures, pictures or writing, and let people know how best to help.

## Emotions

Low mood, anxiety, anger and crying easily are common after a stroke. Tell your GP or stroke team. Talking therapies and support groups can help.

## Driving

You must **not drive for 1 month** after a stroke or TIA. If you still have problems after 1 month, such as weakness, sight or thinking problems, you must tell the **DVLA**. Different rules apply to lorry and bus drivers.

## Staying healthy

Take your medicines, stop smoking, stay active within your limits, eat well and limit alcohol.

## Help at home

Your council can assess your **care needs**, and may arrange equipment or adaptations. See [home adaptations and equipment](/support/home-and-equipment/). Family carers can ask for a **carer's assessment**. See [help for carers](/support/carers/).

## Support

The **Stroke Association** has a free helpline, and **Different Strokes** supports younger stroke survivors.
